---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

// The accessibility page. Same shape as /ai and /privacy: versioned, dated, and
// honest about its own limits rather than claiming a conformance level.
//
// EVERYTHING STATED HERE WAS MEASURED ON 2026-08-22 against the built output of
// both sites, not asserted. The audit covered 27 pages on probablyfine.dev and
// 9 on maxfieldallison.com:
//   - alt text, <html lang>, <main>, skip links, h1 counts, heading-level order
//   - WCAG relative-luminance contrast for every palette token on both the base
//     and surface backgrounds, including the footer's 80% opacity blend
//   - prefers-reduced-motion and :focus-visible handling in global.css
//
// The one real failure is --color-code-comment at 4.42:1. If you change a
// palette token, re-run the numbers and update this page in the same commit.
// Do not raise the claimed level here without an audit that supports it.
const version = 'v1.0';
const updated = '2026-08-22';

const log = [
  {
    version: 'v1.0',
    date: '2026-08-22',
    body: 'First version. Written from an audit of the built output of both sites rather than from intent, which is why it names a specific contrast failure instead of claiming conformance.',
  },
];
---

<Layout title="Accessibility · ProbablyFine" description="What I've actually checked, what currently fails, and what I haven't tested. Measured against the built sites, not claimed.">
  <section class="max-w-[var(--container-shell)] mx-auto px-6 pt-16 pb-8">
    <p class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]">$ cat ./accessibility</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-3">Accessibility</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
      I'm aiming at WCAG 2.2 AA on both of my sites. I'm not claiming I've hit it, because I
      haven't tested this with a screen reader and a page that says "fully conformant" without
      that is guessing. What follows is what I measured, what's broken, and what I haven't looked
      at yet.
    </p>
    <p class="font-mono text-xs text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-4">{version} · updated {updated}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-[var(--container-shell)] mx-auto px-6 pb-8">
    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># what I checked</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        Every built page on both sites, 36 of them. Each one declares its language, has a single
        h1, uses a real <span class="font-mono text-sm">&lt;main&gt;</span> landmark, and offers a
        skip link to it. No heading level is skipped anywhere. Every image has alt text.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Keyboard focus is visible on purpose rather than by browser default, and if your system
        asks for reduced motion the page transitions stop. Things you click are real buttons and
        links, so they work without a mouse.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># what's wrong right now</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        Comments inside code blocks are too faint. They land at 4.42 to 1 against the page
        background, and the standard asks for 4.5 to 1 at that text size. It's a small miss and
        it's still a miss, so it's listed here until the colour changes.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        The small print in the footer passes at 4.56 to 1, which clears the bar by about one part
        in a hundred. That's thinner than I'd like for 11 pixel text and it's on the list to fix
        properly rather than defend.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Everything else in the palette is comfortable. Body text sits at 16 to 1 and the dimmed
        text at 6.6 to 1.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># what I haven't checked</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        A real screen reader. Everything above comes from measuring the markup and the colours,
        which catches the mechanical problems and tells you nothing about whether the thing is
        pleasant to listen to. Until I've done that, treat the AA target as a direction rather
        than a result.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        The comment box is someone else's code running on my page. I picked it and I'm responsible
        for it being there, but I haven't audited it and I can't fix its internals. If it gets in
        your way, tell me, because that's the sort of thing that decides whether it stays.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        The arcade and the ZERO/DAY playtest are the weak spot. The launcher is keyboard operable,
        but what it launches is an emulator and a game, and neither was built with this in mind.
        I'd rather say that than quietly leave them out of scope.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># if something blocks you</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        Email me at{' '}
        <a href="mailto:hello@maxfieldallison.com" class="hover:text-[var(--color-ink)]">hello@maxfieldallison.com</a>
        {' '}and tell me what you were trying to do. You don't need to know the terminology or
        which rule it breaks. "I couldn't get to the thing" is a complete bug report.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        This is a personal site, so it's me reading that mail and me fixing it. No ticket number,
        but also nobody to route around.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># log</h2>
      {
        log.map((e) => (
          <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-ink)]">{e.version} · {e.date}</span> · {e.body}
          </p>
        ))
      }
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        When something here gets fixed, the fix lands in this log and the old entry stays. That's
        the only way you can tell whether the list is getting shorter.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
